.TH CAU-MCP 1 "2026-11-02" "CAU EDR" "CAU EDR User Commands"
.SH NAME
cau\-mcp \- CAU EDR Model Context Protocol server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cau-mcp
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cau-mcp
exposes the same detection engine as
.BR cau (1)
over the Model Context Protocol: JSON-RPC 2.0, by default on standard input and
standard output. An MCP client (an LLM agent or an IDE) drives the engine
through bounded tools (scan, explain, draft and test rules, peel, firmware and
boot-chain inventory, hardware identity, evaluation summaries, coverage and
the honesty index), with file access confined to the directories passed with
\fB\-\-root\fP.
.PP
The surface is analysis only. Nothing it exposes executes, quarantines, blocks
or kills:
.BR "cau defend" ,
.BR "cau net"
and the quarantine store are deliberately not reachable over MCP. Results are
bounded summaries; every list is capped and every cut is reported.
.PP
Resources are resolved with the same discovery rules as the scanner, so a
package install under \fB/usr\fP needs no configuration. The engine behind the
server is the scanner's, not a second implementation: the detectors, thresholds
and report formats are identical.
.PP
Without any \fB\-\-root\fP, the file-touching tools are disabled and only the
hash, rule and coverage tools remain available.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-root " " \fIpath\fP
An allowed scan root for the file-touching tools. Repeatable. With no root,
file tools are disabled. The boot-surface tools (\fBboot_scan\fP,
\fBhardware_identity\fP) accept an efivarfs or sysfs directory only under a
root: the live \fI/sys/firmware/efi/efivars\fP and \fI/sys\fP are reachable
by a client only when the operator passes \fB\-\-root /sys\fP (or the efivars
mount) here.
.TP
.BR \-expect " " \fIfile\fR
Coverage expectation for the honesty index (\fBhonesty_index\fP,
\fIcau://honesty\fP). Defaults to \fIbaselines/attack-coverage.txt\fP when
present in the working directory, as
.BR "cau honesty"
does.
.TP
.BR \-eval " " \fIfile\fR
Evaluation report written by \fBcau eval run \-\-out\fP, for the honesty
index. Defaults to \fIbaselines/smoke.json\fP when present.
.TP
.BR \-manifest " " \fIfile\fR
Integrity baseline for the honesty index. Defaults to \fIintegrity.json\fP
next to the signature database.
.TP
.BR \-pubkey " " \fIfile\fR
Ed25519 public key (hex) the integrity baseline's signature must verify
against; also read from \fB$CAU_SELFTEST_PUBKEY\fP. A key that is supplied
and cannot be read is reported in the index as "signature not checked" with
the reason, never silently dropped.
.TP
.BR \-db " " \fIfile\fR
The hash signature database. Auto-discovered when omitted. Unlike the scanner,
the server treats it as required and exits when it cannot be found.
.TP
.BR \-rules " " \fIfile\fR
The YARA rules file or directory. Auto-discovered when omitted; when YARA rules
are missing, YARA is disabled and the server continues.
.TP
.BR \-fuzzy\-db " " \fIfile\fR
The fuzzy-hash database (TLSH, ssdeep, imphash).
.TP
.BR \-max\-bytes " " \fIn\fR
Per-file size cap for file tools. \fBfirmware_image\fP applies the firmware
analyser's own 64 MiB cap on top of it. Disk images given to \fBboot_scan\fP
are exempt: only their boot sectors and partition tables are read by offset,
never the whole file.
.TP
.BR \-max\-files " " \fIn\fR
Maximum files a single scan_dir call may enumerate.
.TP
.BR \-yara\-timeout " " \fIduration\fR
Per-file YARA scan budget (default 10s).
.TP
.BR \-http " " \fIaddress\fR
Serve the streamable-HTTP transport on this address (for example
\fB127.0.0.1:8923\fP) instead of stdio. Binding to a non-loopback address
exposes the scanner to everyone who can reach it, within the \fB\-\-root\fP
sandbox. A request carrying a browser \fBOrigin\fP other than loopback is refused
with 403, so a web page cannot reach the server through a browser. Clients that
send no \fBOrigin\fP are unaffected.
.TP
.BR \-no\-yara\fR, \fB\-no\-static\fR, \fB\-no\-stego\fR, \fB\-no\-encchain\fR, \fB\-no\-lnk\fR, \fB\-no\-ole\fR, \fB\-no\-pdf\fR, \fB\-no\-iso\fR, \fB\-no\-pkg\fR, \fB\-no\-peel\fR, \fB\-no\-heuristics\fR, \fB\-no\-fusion\fR
Disable one detector. A detector disabled by a flag is reported as disabled, not
silently skipped.
.TP
.BR \-version\fR
Print the version and exit.
.SH TOOLS
Beside the scan, hash, rule and coverage tools, the server exposes these
read-only analyses, each with the same sandbox and bounding as \fBscan_file\fP:
.TP
.B honesty_index
The \fBcau honesty\fP document: coverage against the committed expectation,
what a recorded evaluation measured (with the synthetic-corpus caveat), and
whether the content matches its integrity baseline. No arguments; reads only
the operator paths above.
.TP
.B eval_report
Summarise an evaluation report at a sandboxed path: totals, ratios,
per-detector rows, corpus class, and whether the totals follow from the sample
records. Arguments: \fBpath\fP, \fBinclude_samples\fP, \fBlimit\fP.
.TP
.B peel_file
Peel one file's obfuscation chain: outcome, score, depth, chain signatures,
findings. Decoded bytes are never returned. Argument: \fBpath\fP.
.TP
.B firmware_image
Inventory a UEFI firmware image: volumes, modules, checksum, decompression
counts, findings, and rule matches over module bodies when a ruleset is
loaded. Argument: \fBpath\fP.
.TP
.B boot_scan
Scan explicitly named boot surfaces, each inside a root: \fBdisks\fP (image
files), \fBesp\fP, \fBefivars\fP, \fBsysfs\fP, \fBdbx_reference\fP. No
auto-discovery; signatures are reported present or absent, never verified.
.TP
.B hardware_identity
The \fBcau hwanchor\fP snapshot from a sysfs root inside a root. Arguments:
\fBsysfs\fP, \fBredact\fP (default true: serials, UUID and MAC addresses are
replaced by digests).
.TP
.B mitre_coverage
Takes \fBframeworks\fP (boolean) to add the Unified Kill Chain phase view of
\fBcau coverage \-\-frameworks\fP.
.PP
The resource \fIcau://honesty\fP serves the honesty index without a tool
call, and the prompt \fBboot_chain_review\fP drives a boot-chain triage over
the tools above.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The server ran and shut down cleanly.
.TP
.B 1
A startup failure: the signature database could not be loaded, the sandbox or
an explicit \fB\-\-rules\fP path was invalid, or the transport failed.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was invalid.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/cau/rules/*.yar
Bundled YARA rules.
.TP
.I /usr/share/cau/signatures/hashes.txt
Bundled hash signature database.
.TP
.I /usr/share/cau/signatures/fuzzy.txt
Bundled fuzzy-hash database.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR CAU_SIGNATURES ", " CAU_RULES ", " CAU_FUZZY
Default resource paths, as in
.BR cau (1).
.SH EXAMPLES
Serve to an MCP client, letting it scan a single directory:
.PP
.RS
.nf
cau-mcp \-\-root /srv/uploads \-\-db /usr/share/cau/signatures/hashes.txt
.fi
.RE
